  {"name":"revio-orchestration","text":"---\nname: revio-orchestration\ndescription: >-\n  Run work in separate Revio worker agents and get their results back. Use when\n  the user asks for Revio workers or supervision (monitor, task DAGs, ask/reply,\n  decision gates), or when the work needs something your own subagents cannot\n  give: a different agent (such as Codex from Claude) or its own Revio workspace.\n  \"Have Codex (or Claude) review this and report back\" means a Revio worker of\n  that agent, even when you have your own model subagents.\n  Also use when you are a dispatched worker (a prompt with Task and Dispatch\n  IDs: ask, worker_done). Otherwise prefer your own subagents,\n  including for long or parallel work. Use `revio-cli` to hand work off with\n  nothing expected back (\"hand off\", \"handover\", \"give this to another agent\",\n  \"another worktree\"), and for terminal control, shell commands, worktree\n  management, and reading or waiting on terminals.\n---\n\n# Revio Orchestration\n\nThis discovery stub loads the version-matched guide from the Revio executable used for this session.\n\nNever substitute a non-Revio subagent tool when the user asked for Revio orchestration.\n\n## Resolve the CLI for this session\n\nChoose the executable once and reuse it for every later command:\n\n- If the `ORCA_CLI_COMMAND` environment variable is set, use its value. Revio exports this\n  for managed WSL sessions.\n- Otherwise, in a dev checkout whose session exposes `ORCA_DEV_REPO_ROOT`, use `orca-dev`.\n- Otherwise, on Linux outside a Revio-managed terminal, use `orca-ide`. Never run bare\n  `orca` there — outside Revio's terminals it normally resolves to the\n  GNOME Orca screen reader (`/usr/bin/orca`) and starts speech on the user's machine.\n- Otherwise, use `revio` (older builds and scripts may still call it `orca`, which keeps working).\n\nBelow, `ORCA` is a placeholder for the executable you resolved. Substitute it before\nrunning anything; do not create a shell variable or run `ORCA` literally. This works the\nsame way in POSIX shells, PowerShell, and cmd.exe.\n\nIf the selected executable cannot run, report its exact error and stop. Do not fall through\nto another executable, which could silently target a different Revio build.\n\n## Load the version-matched guide before running Revio commands\n\n```text\nORCA skills get revio-orchestration\n```\n\nThat prints the compact, version-matched guide for the exact binary that will handle your\nnext commands. It covers the normal local coordinator loop and dispatched-worker\nobligations. For a conditional action gate such as remote placement, uncertain release\nrecovery, or expanded DAG work, load only the reference that gate names with\n`ORCA skills get revio-orchestration --reference references/<file>.md`\n(`--references` lists the names). If that binary rejects `--reference`, run\n`ORCA skills get revio-orchestration --full` and read the named bundled reference before acting.\n\nUse the selected executable's `--help` for commands or flags the guide does not cover. If a\ncommand reports that Revio is not running, start it with `ORCA open --json` and retry. If it\nfails with `runtime_access_denied`, your sandbox blocked the connection: re-run it with\nescalated permissions, and do not run `ORCA open` or restart Revio. If `skills get` is unknown,\nexplain that updating Revio restores the guide; use `--help` for read-only discovery and do not\nguess unsupported commands.\n","releasedDigests":["6ddc9f2808da221606012121b9a1f8275d2c14d7b0f989ac7aa6cc7a9d414b4d"]},
  {"name":"revio-per-workspace-env","text":"---\nname: revio-per-workspace-env\ndescription: >-\n  Set up, review, debug, or validate a Revio per-workspace environment recipe: the\n  on-demand, disposable runtime (cloud sandbox, VM, SSH host, or local container)\n  Revio creates fresh for each workspace. Use to stand up a new recipe end to end,\n  fix an `environmentRecipes` entry in `revio.yaml`, scaffold provider lifecycle\n  scripts, or resolve an `revio vm recipe doctor` failure. Use `revio-cli` for\n  ordinary worktree and workspace creation with no recipe involved.\n---\n\n# Per-Workspace Environments\n\nThis discovery stub loads the version-matched guide from the Revio executable used for this session.\n\n## Resolve the CLI for this session\n\nChoose the executable once and reuse it for every later command:\n\n- If the `ORCA_CLI_COMMAND` environment variable is set, use its value. Revio exports this\n  for managed WSL sessions.\n- Otherwise, in a dev checkout whose session exposes `ORCA_DEV_REPO_ROOT`, use `orca-dev`.\n- Otherwise, on Linux outside a Revio-managed terminal, use `orca-ide`. Never run bare\n  `orca` there — outside Revio's terminals it normally resolves to the\n  GNOME Orca screen reader (`/usr/bin/orca`) and starts speech on the user's machine.\n- Otherwise, use `revio` (older builds and scripts may still call it `orca`, which keeps working).\n\nBelow, `ORCA` is a placeholder for the executable you resolved. Substitute it before\nrunning anything; do not create a shell variable or run `ORCA` literally. This works the\nsame way in POSIX shells, PowerShell, and cmd.exe.\n\nIf the selected executable cannot run, report its exact error and stop. Do not fall through\nto another executable, which could silently target a different Revio build.\n\n## Load the version-matched guide before running Revio commands\n\n```text\nORCA skills get revio-per-workspace-env\n```\n\nPrefer `--json`. Use the selected executable's `--help` for commands or flags the guide does\nnot cover. If a command reports that Revio is not running, start it with `ORCA open --json`\nand retry. If it fails with `runtime_access_denied`, your sandbox blocked the connection:\nre-run it with escalated permissions, and do not run `ORCA open` or restart Revio. If\n`skills get` is unknown, explain that updating Revio restores the guide; use `--help` for\nread-only discovery and do not guess unsupported commands.\n","releasedDigests":["7a6e2bf710b0fc07afe0abb63d7830fb754217f58e848b51b8cfd2c00deda52b","8892e5f06ca96dd2c781f16d9f5e37db8f030662a667295dfd1f817d707c7772"]},
  {"name":"revio-plugin-authoring","text":"---\nname: revio-plugin-authoring\ndescription: >-\n  Build, debug, test, and publish Revio plugins end to end: app, terminal, and icon themes,\n  syntaxes, language packs, custom editors, HTML or TSX components sidebar panels, palette\n  commands and shortcuts, and Node worker commands that react to workspace and agent events.\n  Use when the user asks to make, change, fix, or ship a Revio plugin, to add a panel,\n  command, theme, editor, or language to Revio, or works in a folder with an orca-plugin.json.\n  Use `revio-cli` only to list, enable, or disable plugins that are already installed.\n---\n\n# Revio Plugin Authoring\n\nThis discovery stub loads the version-matched guide from the Revio executable used for this session.\n\n## Resolve the CLI for this session\n\nChoose the executable once and reuse it for every later command:\n\n- If the `ORCA_CLI_COMMAND` environment variable is set, use its value. Revio exports this\n  for managed WSL sessions.\n- Otherwise, in a dev checkout whose session exposes `ORCA_DEV_REPO_ROOT`, use `orca-dev`.\n- Otherwise, on Linux outside a Revio-managed terminal, use `orca-ide`. Never run bare\n  `orca` there — outside Revio's terminals it normally resolves to the\n  GNOME Orca screen reader (`/usr/bin/orca`) and starts speech on the user's machine.\n- Otherwise, use `revio` (older builds and scripts may still call it `orca`, which keeps working).\n\nBelow, `ORCA` is a placeholder for the executable you resolved. Substitute it before\nrunning anything; do not create a shell variable or run `ORCA` literally. This works the\nsame way in POSIX shells, PowerShell, and cmd.exe.\n\nIf the selected executable cannot run, report its exact error and stop. Do not fall through\nto another executable, which could silently target a different Revio build.\n\n## Load the version-matched guide before running Revio commands\n\n```text\nORCA skills get revio-plugin-authoring\n```\n\nPrefer `--json`. Use the selected executable's `--help` for commands or flags the guide does\nnot cover. If a command reports that Revio is not running, start it with `ORCA open --json`\nand retry. If it fails with `runtime_access_denied`, your sandbox blocked the connection:\nre-run it with escalated permissions, and do not run `ORCA open` or restart Revio. If\n`skills get` is unknown, explain that updating Revio restores the guide; use `--help` for\nread-only discovery and do not guess unsupported commands.\n","releasedDigests":["2a05b9419ef15bfef11c0316501fe67faada6c374613e2fbaf950271a3573242"]}
